title: The Upcountry
subtitle: South Carolina Interior in Rebellion
tags: event
new-page: true
view-if: SC_gub_winner = "rep" and enforcement_acts = 1 and habeas_suspended != 1 and SC_kkk_strength + SC_whiteL_strength >= 100
max-visits: 1
on-arrival: {!
    Q.SC_kkk_strength = (Q.SC_kkk_strength || 0) + 4;
    Q.kkk_strength += 1;
    Q.SC_freemen_rep -= 3;
    Q.SC_rural_dem += 1;
!}

= The Upcountry

The reports reaching Columbia from the South Carolina upcountry have become impossible to ignore. In several districts, Republican meetings have ceased altogether, militia companies have resigned or disappeared. Those who still hold office travel with armed escorts, and witnesses to assaults have begun leaving their homes rather than appear before a grand jury.

The trouble has reached the governor's own administration. He has spent months asking Washington for help, insisting that the state courts cannot be trusted to prosecute the men responsible. The Attorney General has now sent investigators, accompanied by an Army officer, to examine the situation firsthand.

Their findings are severe. Local grand juries routinely refuse to indict suspects, while sheriffs warn witnesses against appearing in court. The investigators describe an organized campaign to make Republican government impossible outside the larger towns. They recommend federal intervention under the Enforcement Acts.

Congress has given the President authority to suspend habeas corpus in areas where unlawful combinations are preventing the enforcement of federal law. Such a suspension would permit the authorities to hold suspected conspirators while the government dismantles their organization. It would also place the administration in direct conflict with those who regard the measure as an abuse of executive power.

[? if SC_occupation_posture >= 1 : [+ SC_garrison_troops +] thousand federal troops are stationed in South Carolina. Their cooperation will determine how quickly the order can be enforced. ?][? if SC_occupation_posture < 1 : No federal troops are stationed in South Carolina. Any proclamation we issue will be difficult to enforce without a military presence. ?]

- @warn_first: Issue a proclamation ordering the Klan to disperse
- @suspend_now: Suspend habeas corpus at once

@warn_first
on-arrival: {!
    Q.SC_kkk_strength = (Q.SC_kkk_strength || 0) + 3;
    Q.liberal_dissent = Math.max(0, (Q.liberal_dissent || 0) - 2);
    Q.dem_relation -= 1;
!}
go-to: warning_heeded if SC_garrison_troops >= 10 and kkk_militancy < 0.4; warning_ignored if SC_garrison_troops < 10 or kkk_militancy >= 0.4

We issue a proclamation requiring armed organizations in the affected districts to disband, surrender their weapons and abandon their disguises by a specified deadline. Federal marshals distribute copies throughout the region, and the governor orders local officials to post the notice in every courthouse.

The proclamation gives the suspects time to disperse. It also makes the consequences of defiance unmistakable. Once the deadline expires, the administration will have to decide whether its authority extends beyond the printed page.

@suspend_now
choose-if: army_loyalty >= 0.5 and SC_occupation_posture >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: [? if SC_occupation_posture < 1 : We have no federal presence in South Carolina to carry it out. ?][? if army_loyalty < 0.5 : The army cannot be relied on for this operation. ?]
on-arrival: {!
    let troops = Q.SC_garrison_troops || 0;
    Q.habeas_suspended = 1;
    Q.SC_kkk_strength = Math.max(0, (Q.SC_kkk_strength || 0) - Math.min(45, 20 + troops * 1.5));
    Q.SC_whiteL_strength = Math.max(0, (Q.SC_whiteL_strength || 0) - Math.min(15, 5 + troops * 0.5));
    Q.kkk_strength = Math.max(0, Q.kkk_strength * 0.85);
    Q.kkk_militancy += 0.06;
    Q.SC_freemen_rep += 3;
    Q.SC_rural_dem += 5;
    Q.SC_middle_class_dem += 2;
    Q.radical_strength += 2;
    Q.liberal_dissent += 8;
    Q.moderate_dissent += 4;
    Q.conservative_dissent += 5;
    Q.dem_relation -= 10;
    Q.reconstruction_support -= 2;
!}
go-to: trials

We order the immediate arrest of suspected leaders and authorize the suspension of habeas corpus in the affected districts. Federal troops and marshals begin coordinated raids, seizing weapons, correspondence and lists of suspected Republican opponents. Some suspects flee before the columns arrive; others surrender when they learn that the government intends to hold them.

The operation produces results, but the legal and political objections are immediate. <span style="color: #DAA520;">**Liberal Republicans**</span> question whether the administration has gone too far, while <span style="color: #0015bc;">**Democrats**</span> denounce the arrests as an executive assault on civil liberty. The governor insists that ordinary court proceedings had become a shield for organized violence.

@warning_heeded
on-arrival: {!
    Q.SC_kkk_strength = Math.max(0, (Q.SC_kkk_strength || 0) - 12);
    Q.SC_freemen_rep += 2;
    Q.kkk_militancy += 0.02;
    Q.dem_relation -= 2;
!}

The deadline passes without a general confrontation. In several districts, armed bands dissolve and their members surrender their disguises and weapons to federal marshals. Local officials report that some of the leading men have left their homes, while others have quietly returned to ordinary life.

[? if SC_garrison_troops >= 10 : The presence of federal troops appears to have persuaded many suspects that the proclamation will be enforced. ?] The governor reports a marked decline in violence, though he cautions that the organization has not been destroyed. The administration has gained time without resorting to mass arrests. Whether the peace will survive the withdrawal of the soldiers remains uncertain.

@warning_ignored

The deadline expires without any meaningful surrender. Reports from the upcountry describe the same nightly meetings and armed patrols as before. In some districts, the proclamation has been mocked publicly; in others, local officials have simply refused to post it.

[? if SC_garrison_troops < 10 : The small federal garrison has done little to convince the suspects that the government can enforce its order. ?][? if SC_garrison_troops >= 10 : Despite the troops already in the state, the Klan appears confident that Washington will not authorize a sustained campaign of arrests. ?]

The governor now demands that we act on the authority Congress has provided. Another warning would leave the administration with little credibility. We must either suspend habeas corpus and begin arrests or accept that the proclamation will go unenforced.

- @suspend_after_warning: Suspend habeas corpus
- @stand_down: Let the warning stand

@suspend_after_warning
choose-if: army_loyalty >= 0.5 and SC_occupation_posture >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: [? if SC_occupation_posture < 1 : We have no federal presence in South Carolina to carry it out. ?][? if army_loyalty < 0.5 : The army cannot be relied on for this operation. ?]
on-arrival: {!
    let troops = Q.SC_garrison_troops || 0;
    Q.habeas_suspended = 1;
    Q.SC_kkk_strength = Math.max(0, (Q.SC_kkk_strength || 0) - Math.min(45, 20 + troops * 1.5));
    Q.SC_whiteL_strength = Math.max(0, (Q.SC_whiteL_strength || 0) - Math.min(15, 5 + troops * 0.5));
    Q.kkk_strength = Math.max(0, Q.kkk_strength * 0.85);
    Q.kkk_militancy += 0.04;
    Q.SC_freemen_rep += 3;
    Q.SC_rural_dem += 4;
    Q.SC_middle_class_dem += 1;
    Q.radical_strength += 2;
    Q.liberal_dissent += 3;
    Q.moderate_dissent += 2;
    Q.conservative_dissent += 3;
    Q.dem_relation -= 6;
    Q.reconstruction_support -= 1;
!}
go-to: trials

We authorize the suspension and begin the arrests. The administration can now point to the failed proclamation as evidence that the ordinary means of enforcement have been exhausted. Federal marshals and soldiers move against the suspected leaders, and the governor's office prepares lists of prisoners for prosecution.

The delay has given some suspects time to escape, but the government has established a basis for action that was absent when the first reports arrived. <span style="color: #0015bc;">**Democrats**</span> continue to denounce the policy, while supporters argue that the administration gave its opponents an opportunity to comply and received defiance in return.

@stand_down
on-arrival: {!
    Q.SC_kkk_strength = (Q.SC_kkk_strength || 0) + 6;
    Q.SC_freemen_rep -= 5;
    Q.SC_rural_dem += 3;
    Q.Union_League_strength = Math.max(0, Q.Union_League_strength - 8);
    Q.freemen_rep -= 3;
    Q.radical_dissent += 5;
    Q.dem_relation += 3;
!}

We decline to take further action. The proclamation remains in force, but no one expects the Regulators of the upcountry to obey it. Within days, reports of renewed intimidation reach the governor's office.

The decision draws sharp criticism from <span style="color: #7A1F5C;">**Radicals**</span>, who argue that we have exposed the limits of federal protection. The governor is left to administer a law that his own officers cannot enforce, while local Republicans begin to question whether Washington will intervene at all.

@trials

The arrests overwhelm the local authorities. Jail cells fill, federal marshals struggle to keep track of the prisoners, and prosecutors must decide which cases can be brought first. Some suspects confess to attending meetings or participating in raids. Others deny involvement and insist that the government has arrested them for their political beliefs.

The central problem is now the courts. Years of intimidation have left prosecutors with few willing witnesses, while the number of suspects far exceeds the capacity of the district courts to try them promptly. The Attorney General's office recommends concentrating on the principal organizers, but Republican officials in South Carolina warn that releasing the rank and file will allow the organization to recover.

We must decide how to handle the prisoners.

- @leniency: Release those who confess and prosecute the leaders
- @prosecute_all: Try every man we have arrested

@leniency
on-arrival: {!
    Q.SC_kkk_strength = Math.max(0, (Q.SC_kkk_strength || 0) - 10);
    Q.SC_freemen_rep += 1;
    Q.SC_rural_dem = Q.SC_rural_dem - 2;
    Q.radical_dissent += 3;
    Q.liberal_dissent = Math.max(0, (Q.liberal_dissent || 0) - 2);
!}

We release the men who confess to lesser offenses and concentrate our resources on the alleged organizers. The decision eases the burden on the courts and allows prosecutors to focus on the cases most likely to dismantle the organization.

The policy divides the Republican coalition. <span style="color: #7A1F5C;">**Radicals**</span> argue that men who participated in the campaign of terror have been allowed to escape punishment. The administration replies that a smaller number of convictions against the leaders will do more lasting damage than hundreds of cases the courts cannot finish.

@prosecute_all
choose-if: budget >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: [? if budget < 1 : We cannot afford that many trials. ?]
on-arrival: {!
    Q.budget -= 1;
    Q.SC_kkk_strength = Math.max(0, (Q.SC_kkk_strength || 0) - 6);
    Q.SC_freemen_rep += 3;
    Q.SC_rural_dem += 6;
    Q.SC_middle_class_dem += 2;
    Q.radical_strength += 3;
    Q.liberal_dissent += 3;
    Q.dem_relation -= 4;
    Q.PoliticalCostPenalty += 1;
!}

We order prosecutors to pursue every case. The district courts remain occupied for months as defendants appear one after another. Many plead guilty rather than face trial, while others are convicted on testimony that local juries would once have refused to hear.

The process is expensive and slow, and not every sentence reflects the severity of the crimes. Still, for the first time, men who relied on local impunity face imprisonment under federal law. <span style="color: #0015bc;">**Democrats**</span> denounce the prosecutions as political vengeance, while the governor's supporters point to the convictions as proof that federal intervention was necessary.